<h2>How the case travelled</h2>
<p>The dispute&rsquo;s procedural journey is itself a map of India&rsquo;s award-review structure. The sole arbitrator decided nine claims arising from the 2008 Coal Mining and Delivery Agreement, by an award of 15 January 2021 and an additional award of 5 July 2021. The Commercial Court, Jaipur, acting under Section 34, set aside the tribunal&rsquo;s findings on several claims and the additional award on limitation grounds — while correctly acknowledging it had no power to modify the award. The Rajasthan High Court, acting under Section 37, held on 21 May 2026 that the Commercial Court had overstepped, and restored both awards. The Supreme Court was the third — and most reluctant — filter.</p>

<h2>The seven working principles</h2>
<p>The judgment distils the review framework into principles the Court then applies. In summary form:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sparing interference.</strong> Article 136 is &ldquo;to be exercised sparingly, only where exceptional circumstances exist&rdquo; — especially after an award has attained finality at the Section 37 stage.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No appeal over awards.</strong> A Section 34 court &ldquo;does not sit in appeal over the award&rdquo;; it applies only the statutory grounds, including patent illegality that goes to the root.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Contract-reading belongs to the tribunal.</strong> Under Section 28(3), construing the agreement is primarily the arbitrator&rsquo;s task; an interpretation &ldquo;is not to be interfered with merely because another view is possible.&rdquo;</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 37 is narrower still.</strong> The appellate court checks the Section 34 court&rsquo;s compliance with its own limits; it does not reappraise evidence to record contrary findings.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court anchored these in the established line — Associate Builders v. DDA, (2015) 3 SCC 49; Ssangyong v. NHAI, (2019) 15 SCC 131; Patel Engineering v. NEEPCO, (2020) 7 SCC 167; DMRC v. Delhi Airport Metro Express, (2024) 6 SCC 357; and the recent Jan De Nul Dredging India v. Tuticorin Port Trust, (2026) 3 SCC 186, whose observation that interference with arbitral matters &ldquo;is virtually prohibited, if not absolutely barred&rdquo; sets the judgment&rsquo;s tone.</p>

<h2>The three questions the Court asked</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Were the tribunal&rsquo;s findings plausible?</strong> For each claim: was the view taken a possible one, free of patent illegality?</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Did the Section 34 court stay in bounds?</strong> Or did it substitute its own appreciation of evidence and contract for the arbitrator&rsquo;s?</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Did the High Court exercise Section 37 correctly?</strong> Restoration of an award is proper where the Section 34 court exceeded its jurisdiction.</div>
</div>
<p>Answering that framework against the record, the Court found no exceptional circumstance warranting Article 136 interference with the High Court&rsquo;s restoration of the awards.</p>

<h2>What award-holders and challengers should take away</h2>
<p>For award-holders, the judgment strengthens an already strong hand: each successive forum applies a narrower lens, and by the time a matter reaches the Supreme Court, the challenger must show something exceptional — not merely that the arbitrator could have read the contract differently. For challengers, the discipline is to build the entire case within Section 34&rsquo;s vocabulary at the first opportunity: no evidence, ignored vital evidence, a view no reasonable person could take, conflict with the contract&rsquo;s express terms. Arguments framed as appellate grievances — the quantum is too high, the interpretation is wrong — are structurally doomed, and the cost of pursuing them through three filters is rarely recovered.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information only and does not comment on any pending proceedings between the parties.</div>
